#import "style.typ": *
#front[How to read this book]

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Starting from the prime-counting question]
Read the three opening sections of Chapter 1 together. The staircase of prime counts leads to Euler's product, Riemann's count of prime powers, and the line on which the zeros are conjectured to lie. The pair $rho,overline(rho)$ in #link(<c01s03>)[§1.3] is the first calculation to carry forward. It predicts both the size and the frequency of an oscillation in the variable $log x$. The remainder of the chapter explains how much information the different counting functions contain.

In Chapter 2 follow the successive historical questions. The prime number theorem in #link(<c02s01>)[§2.1] locates a zero-free boundary. Hardy's argument and the later proportion theorems in #link(<c02s03>)[§2.3] find zeros on the line. The point counts in #link(<c02s05>)[§2.5] give a setting in which a square-root estimate can be proved by arithmetic. The route described in #link(<c02s08>)[§2.8] brings the two lines of inquiry together. It uses arithmetic divisibility to bound a sum whose size is sensitive to one exceptional zero. The interlude after Chapter 6 calculates the residues in the explicit formula and the gamma contribution to the zero count. It identifies the classical estimates used in passing to limits.

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Four tools, each with a task in the proof]
*Complex analysis.* Work through the transform of a single power in #link(<c03s01>)[§3.1] and the Gaussian functional equation in #link(<c03s10>)[§3.10]. The first turns growth into a pole. The second explains why eliminating zeros strictly to the right of one half will suffice. As a check, write down what a zero $0.7+3i$ would contribute together with its conjugate to the count.

*Arithmetic Fourier analysis.* The two orthogonality relations in #link(<c04s02>)[§4.2] invert the prime statistics in two different directions. The example in #link(<c04s09>)[§4.9] explains what changes when the prime ramifies. Together they supply the linear algebra for designing the family in Chapter 10 and recovering it in Chapter 17.

*Lattices and periods.* Compute the covolume of an index-$p$ sublattice in #link(<c05s03>)[§5.3]. Then see a period calculated in two orders in #link(<c05s11>)[§5.11]. These calculations distinguish local divisibility from the size of a vector over the reals. Comparing both for the same integral map is the work of Chapters 12–14.

*Galois cohomology.* In #link(<c06s03>)[§6.3] the upper-right entry of a matrix satisfies the cocycle law. Changing the splitting adds a coboundary. A global class may disappear after restriction to specified local groups. #link(<c06s08>)[§6.8] explains why the size of the resulting kernel can enter an estimate for congruences. #link(<c06s12>)[§6.12] then follows one target from these tools into the proof.

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Following the proof for the first time]
Begin Part II by keeping a single proposed zero in mind. Chapter 8 constructs a real sum of prime-power terms whose Mellin transform has a pole at that zero. Chapter 9 forces a positive excursion on every sufficiently late interval of a prescribed logarithmic width. Read #link(<c09s06>)[§9.6] before entering the arithmetic chapters. It states precisely which upper bound would give a contradiction.

One may then read Chapter 10, #link(<c14s08>)[§14.8], #link(<c16s13>)[§16.13], and #link(<c18s04>)[§18.4] to see how a bound from arithmetic contradicts the excursion forced by a zero. On a detailed reading, follow Chapters 11–17 in order. At the end of each chapter identify the field, the scale and the object being averaged. For example, a word in a chain of theta lifts has a different role from a group element translating its weights. The two operations meet in #link(<c17s06>)[§17.6]. Chapter 18 returns to the function $pi(x)$ from page one of the historical narrative.

The exercises begin with seven short problems following the whole argument. The subsequent problems give longer calculations using the tools of particular chapters. Appendix C is another entry point for readers working with the public source. Its statements expose the hypotheses of the deep comparison theorems used in the exposition.

